# Airgapped k0rdent Enterprise {{.KcmVersion}} — Install Runbook

Generated by **airgapdeploy**. This runbook and the accompanying scripts encode
the Mirantis airgap install procedure for your specific configuration:

| Setting              | Value |
|----------------------|-------|
| k0rdent Enterprise   | `{{.KcmVersion}}` |
| k0s                  | `{{.K0sVersion}}` |
| Architectures        | `{{range $i, $a := .Architectures}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}{{$a}}{{end}}` |
| Private registry     | `{{.Registry}}` |
| Charts (OCI)         | `{{.ChartsRepoURL}}` |
| k0s fileserver URL   | `{{.FileserverURL}}` |
| Install namespace    | `{{.Namespace}}` |
| TLS mode             | `{{.TLSMode}}`{{if .UsesCustomTLS}} — secret `{{.CACertSecretName}}` from `{{.CACertPath}}`{{end}} |

The install has **two sides**. Do them in order.

---

## Side A — Prep machine (HAS internet)

A machine with internet access that can also reach your private registry.

**Tooling:** bash ≥4.2, GNU coreutils, tar ≥1.34, wget, docker (runs the bundled
skopeo), cosign ≥2.4.1.

1. Copy `01-prepare.sh` to the prep machine.
2. Run it:
   ```bash
   ./01-prepare.sh
   ```
   It downloads and cosign-verifies the airgap bundle, pushes all images and
   charts to `{{.Registry}}`, and downloads + verifies the k0s binaries
   ({{range $i, $a := .Architectures}}{{if $i}}, {{end}}`{{$.K0sBinaryPrefix}}-{{$a}}`{{end}}).
3. Move the downloaded k0s binaries into the airgap (USB, internal transfer,
   etc.) alongside the `02-fileserver/` directory.

> **Do not rename the k0s binary.** Child cluster deployment depends on the
> exact filename `{{.PrimaryK0sBinary}}`.

---

## Side B — Airgapped management cluster

**Tooling:** kubectl + helm ≥3.16.3 against a management cluster (Kubernetes
≥1.30) with a `StorageClass` and a LoadBalancer provider.

### 1. Stand up the k0s fileserver
```bash
kubectl apply -f 02-fileserver/namespace-pvc.yaml
kubectl apply -f 02-fileserver/binary-loader.yaml
# copy the k0s binary into the PVC (expects the binary in the current dir):
./02-fileserver/load-k0s.sh
kubectl apply -f 02-fileserver/configmap-nginx.yaml
kubectl apply -f 02-fileserver/deployment-service.yaml
```

Get the external IP and confirm the binary is served:
```bash
kubectl get svc k0s-fileserver -n {{.FileserverNamespace}} --watch
EXTERNAL_IP="$(kubectl get svc k0s-fileserver -n {{.FileserverNamespace}} -o jsonpath='{.status.loadBalancer.ingress[0].ip}')"
curl -I "http://${EXTERNAL_IP}/{{.RegistryProject}}/{{.PrimaryK0sBinary}}"
```
Expect `HTTP/1.1 200 OK`. Make sure this URL matches `fileserverURL`
(`{{.FileserverURL}}`) in your config — update `03-values.yaml` if the
LoadBalancer IP differs from what you configured.

### 2. Validate access before installing
Prove the registry and every node can reach, trust, and pull images:
```bash
./check-access.sh                     # registry reachable/trusted, images present, fileserver up
./check-node-pull/run.sh              # DaemonSet pulls a canary image on EVERY node
```
`check-access.sh` exits non-zero if anything fails. `check-node-pull/run.sh`
reports per-node pull results and, on failure, prints the offending node and
events. Fix any failures here before installing — they are the same failures
that would otherwise surface mid-install as `ImagePullBackOff`.

### 3. Install k0rdent Enterprise
```bash
./04-install.sh
```
This runs `helm install kcm {{.ChartsRepoURL}}/k0rdent-enterprise --version {{.KcmVersion}} -n {{.Namespace}} --create-namespace -f 03-values.yaml`.

### 4. Verify (wait 5–10 minutes first)
```bash
./05-verify.sh
```
Expect ~30 pods `Running` in `{{.Namespace}}`, ~10 in `projectsveltos`,
`Management` READY = `True`, and all templates VALID.

### Troubleshooting — an image will not pull or install
```bash
./diagnose.sh
```
Surfaces image-pull/crash errors (with the offending image, node, and events),
Helm release status, and any templates that are not valid, then lists the most
likely causes. Re-run `./check-access.sh` to re-test registry/fileserver access.
{{- if .UsesCustomTLS}}

> **Node CA trust.** The `{{.CACertSecretName}}` Kubernetes secret makes the
> *controller* trust the registry, but each node's **container runtime**
> (containerd) must ALSO trust the CA to pull images. If `check-node-pull`
> fails with x509/certificate errors, add the CA to the nodes' containerd
> registry config (e.g. a `hosts.toml` / `ca_file` for `{{.RegistryHost}}`) and
> re-test.
{{- end}}

---

## Notes

- **Non-authenticated registry.** These artifacts assume the registry does not
  require authentication (the v1.4.0 default). If yours does, you will also need
  image-pull and registry-credential secrets plus extra `values.yaml` keys — not
  generated here.
{{- if .UsesCustomTLS}}
- **TLS ({{.TLSModeLabel}}).** Before install, `04-install.sh` creates the
  `{{.CACertSecretName}}` secret from `{{.CACertPath}}` (override with
  `CA_CERT_PATH`) and `03-values.yaml` sets `registryCertSecret`{{if .FileserverIsHTTPS}} and `k0sURLCertSecret`{{end}}.
  Provide {{if eq .TLSMode "self-signed"}}the registry's self-signed certificate{{else}}the CA bundle that signed the registry certificate{{end}} as `{{.CACertPath}}`
  (PEM). Copy it into the airgap alongside the scripts. The same file is also
  needed on the **prep machine** — `01-prepare.sh` mounts it into skopeo and
  pushes with TLS verification enabled (`--dest-cert-dir`), so the registry cert
  is validated against your CA rather than trusted blindly.
{{- else if .UsesACME}}
- **TLS (automated ACME).** Certificates are issued and renewed automatically by
  cert-manager (installed with k0rdent), so there is no CA file to manage and no
  `registryCertSecret` — the issued certs are publicly trusted. After the kcm
  install (step 3), apply the issuer and certificate:
  ```bash
  kubectl apply -f tls-acme/clusterissuer.yaml
  kubectl apply -f tls-acme/certificate.yaml
  ```
  Then point your registry / fileserver **ingress** at the `registry-serving-tls`
  secret cert-manager produces. Issuer: `{{.ACMEIssuerName}}`, ACME server
  `{{.ACMEServer}}`, HTTP-01 solver{{if .ACMEIngressClass}} via ingress class `{{.ACMEIngressClass}}`{{end}}.

  > **AIR-GAP WARNING.** Public Let's Encrypt needs outbound reach to the ACME
  > server AND inbound reach from it to your ingress for the HTTP-01 challenge —
  > which a fully isolated air-gap does not have. For a true air-gap, set the
  > ACME server to an **internal** endpoint (step-ca, Vault, or a Windows CA with
  > the ACME role). `check-access.sh`/the Configure page will flag if the ACME
  > server is unreachable.
{{- else}}
- **TLS (none).** No custom CA is configured; the registry is expected to be
  plain HTTP or to present a publicly-trusted certificate. To trust a
  self-signed or internal-CA registry, choose that TLS mode and regenerate.
{{- end}}
- **Offline cosign.** `01-prepare.sh` runs on the internet-connected prep
  machine, so signature verification uses the online transparency log. For fully
  offline verification, fetch `cosign.pub` separately and consult the Mirantis
  docs for the current offline flag.
- **Image coverage.** Cross-check pushed images against Mirantis's
  `full_images_list.txt` for your version:
  `{{.BundleBaseURL}}/{{.KcmVersion}}/full_images_list.txt`
